# Citation metadata. GitHub renders a "Cite this repository" button from this
# file, in APA and BibTeX, so anyone referencing AdaptShot has one canonical
# form to copy rather than inventing their own.
#
# `version` and `date-released` must track the release. tests/test_citation.py
# checks the version against pyproject.toml, because a stale citation is worse
# than none -- it looks authoritative while pointing at software that no longer
# exists.
#
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "AdaptShot: human-aligned few-shot vision learning for resource-constrained environments"
message: "If you use this software, please cite it using the metadata from this file."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: "Johnson Christopher"
    family-names: "Hassan"
    email: "johnson2006christopher@gmail.com"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-2546-7549"
repository-code: "https://github.com/johnson2006christopher/adaptshot"
url: "https://johnson2006christopher.github.io/adaptshot/"
abstract: >-
  AdaptShot is a CPU-first, human-in-the-loop few-shot vision library. It learns
  a classifier from a handful of labelled examples per class, reports conformal
  prediction sets rather than bare confidence scores, and accepts corrections
  from a person without retraining. It is built for settings where connectivity,
  hardware and labelled data are all scarce.
keywords:
  - few-shot learning
  - computer vision
  - conformal prediction
  - human-in-the-loop
  - uncertainty quantification
  - edge computing
  - CPU inference
license: MIT
version: 0.3.0
date-released: "2026-08-29"
